y atrial fibrillation?\n[patient] i have.\n[doctor] okay. and how are you doing with that?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay. i\'ve been taking my metoprolol 25 milligrams twice a day and it\'s been fine.\n[doctor] okay. and how are you doing with your acid reflux?\n[patient] i\'ve been doing well. i\'ve been taking my protonix 40 milligrams once a day and it\'s been very helpful.\n[doctor] okay. and how are you doing with that?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] okay. so, let\'s go ahead and do a quick physical exam. so, looking at you, you look in no distress. your thyroid is a little bit enlarged but nontender. there are no carotid bruits. your lungs are clear bilaterally. your heart has an irregular rate and rhythm. your abdomen is nice and soft and you have no edema in your lower extremities.\n[patient] mm-hmm.\n[doctor] so, i reviewed your lab results and your glucose was elevated and your hemoglobin a1c was elevated as well. so, let\'s talk a little bit about my assessment and plan for you. so, for your first problem of high blood sugar, i\'d like to order a complete metabolic panel to further evaluate. i\'m going to start you on metformin 500 milligrams twice a day. i\'d like you to continue watching your diet and we\'ll give you a glucometer so you can monitor your glucose levels at home. does that sound good?\n[patient] yeah, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. and then for your second problem of atrial fibrillation, i\'m going to continue you on metoprolol 25 milligrams twice a day. i\'ll refill that for you today. and then for your third problem of reflux, i\'m going to continue you on protonix 40 milligrams a day. does that all sound good?\n[patient] yeah, that sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. all right. i\'ll have the nurse come in and get you checked out. i\'ll see you back in a couple of weeks.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] all right. take care.\n[patient] thank you.\n[doctor] you\'re welcome. }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Reference Answer:\n[patient] um , i have high blood sugar . yeah , osteoarthritis , arterial fibrillation , and reflux .\n[doctor] hi , hannah . how are you ?\n[patient] good , how are you ?\n[doctor] i\'m good . you ready to get started ?\n[patient] yes , i am .\n[doctor] so , hannah is a , uh , 52-year-old female here for evaluation of a high blood sugar . so , hannah , what ha- what happened ? i heard you were in the emergency room with a high blood sugar .\n[patient] yes . so , i\'ve been eating a ton of sweets recently . with the holidays it just feels like there is always something sweet available. whether i am at work and there are holiday gift baskets or clients bringing in treats or when i visit my family for the holidays and there is every baked good imaginable, there is always something sweet i can be eating. in the beginning i felt fine, but i have started to feel it after a couple of weeks, and this past week i just felt really light-headed and i could ... was seeing spots , really dizzy . so i went into the emergency room and they said i had a high blood sugar . um , so i wanted to come in and follow up with you to get that , um , get checked out .\n[doctor] okay . all right . and how , how are you feeling now ? did they , did , did they treat you in the emergency room ? did they gi- they give you some iv fluids and things like that ?\n[patient] yeah . they gave me some fluids and they told me just to try to really cut out eating any extra sweets and get back into my normal routine and diet, but i am still staying with my family, and my mom and grandmother are big bakers so i think it will be really hard to get into a routine there so i just want to make sure i am nipping this high blood sugar thing in the bud.\n[doctor] okay . and i saw you went through a review of systems sheet with a nurse . i just want to ask you a few more questions from that standpoint . so you have had any fever or chills ?\n[patient] no .\n[doctor] any chest pain or shortness of breath ?\n[patient] n- uh , no .\n[doctor] abdomi