-d '{'model': 'gpt-4o', 'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are a fair judge assistant assigned to deliver insightful feedback that compares individual performances, highlighting how each stands relative to others within the same cohort.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of two responses strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, choose a better response between Response A and Response B. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: "(write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (A or B)"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###Instruction:\n\nImagine you are a medical professional tasked with evaluating simulated doctor-patient conversations generated from summary notes. \nThese conversations should accurately reconstruct interactions based on the SOAP (Subjective, Objective, Assessment, Plan) format provided in the notes.\n\nHere is the medical note:\n#############################\nCHIEF COMPLAINT\n\nRight knee injury.\n\nHISTORY OF PRESENT ILLNESS\n\nScott Miller is a pleasant 36-year-old male who presents to the clinic today for the evaluation of a right knee injury. The injury occurred 3 days ago, when he was playing pickup soccer games and felt a pop in his right knee. He reports swelling at the time of the injury, which has improved. The patient states that his knee is not as functional or moving as well as it used to be. He rates his pain level as a 6 to 7 out of 10 currently and a 4 to 5 out of 10 when the swelling decreases. The patient has been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis, which provides some relief. He states that he is able to bend his knee with pain towards the end of this motion; however, when he goes to straighten his knee, he feels like he has to help it straight. The patient adds that he is able to bear weight on his right knee, however it feels loose and does not feel normal.\n\nSOCIAL HISTORY\n\nThe patient works at a desk.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports right knee pain.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Marked limp in the right leg upon ambulation.\nMSK: Examination of the right knee: Significant effusion. Slight ecchymosis. Pain with palpation at the medial joint line. Difficulty with passive ROM. Lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to 115 degrees. Lacking 20-30 degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain. Positive Lachman\'s test. Muscle strength is 5/5. Neurovascular intact distally.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nRight knee pain, possible anterior cruciate ligament tear.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient in regards to his current symptoms. I have recommended that we obtain an MRI of the right knee to evaluate the integrity of the ACL. I have prescribed the patient meloxicam 15 mg a day to reduce swelling and pain. We discussed that the patient should utilize ice and activity modification to reduce swelling and pain. I recommended that he wears a knee immobilizer and use crutches when he is ambulating. He will avoid jogging until we have the results of his MRI.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me once the MRI results are available for review and further discussion.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hey scott good to see you today so i see that you\'re here for a right knee injury can you tell me a little bit about how that happened\n[patient] yeah so i was playing soccer with some friends saturday afternoon and i felt a pop in my knee and then i noticed some swelling but i\'m able to walk around a little bit but it\'s not moving right\n[doctor] okay and how long ago did this happen\n[patient] so that was three days ago\n[doctor] okay so three days ago playing soccer and you felt a pop in your knee and you noticed some swelling at the time of the injury has that swelling improved at all\n[patient] yeah the swelling\'s gotten a little bit better but it\'s still there\n[doctor] okay and how would you rate your pain on a scale of one to ten with ten being the worst pain you\'ve ever felt in your life\n[patient] right now it\'s probably a six or seven out of ten\n[doctor] okay and then when the swelling goes down does it get any better\n[patient] yeah it goes down to like a four or five out of ten\n[doctor] okay and are you doing anything for the pain\n[patient] i\'ve been taking ibuprofen on a regular basis and it does help\n[doctor] okay and are you able to bend your knee at all\n[patient] yeah i can bend it but it hurts towards the end of the motion\n[doctor] okay and then when you go to straighten your knee do you feel like you have to help it straighten\n[patient] yeah i do\n[doctor] okay and are you able to bear weight on it at all\n[patient] yeah i can but it feels loose and it\'s not normal\n[doctor] okay so are you able to walk around on it\n[patient] yeah i can but i have to limp\n[doctor] okay and do you work at a desk or are you on your feet a lot\n[patient] i work at a desk\n[doctor] okay so you\'re not on your feet a lot then\n[patient] no i\'m not\n[doctor] okay and have you had any other symptoms like numbness or tingling in your leg\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and have you had any other injuries to that knee in the past\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and how about any other joints have you injured those\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay so any other joints in the past\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other medical problems\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and are you taking any other medications\n[patient] just the ibuprofen\n[doctor] okay and do you have any allergies\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay and do you have any other symptoms aside from the knee pain\n[patient] no\n[doctor] okay so let\'s go ahead and do a physical exam of your knee i\'m just gon na check out your knee here i\'m gon na start by looking at your gait so can you walk across the room for me\n[patient] sure\n[doctor] okay i do see a marked limp in your right leg and now i\'m gon na examine your knee i\'m gon na feel around here and you\'re going to let me know if you feel any pain\n[patient] okay\n[doctor] okay there is significant effusion there is some ecchymosis here there is pain with palpation at the medial joint line there is difficulty with passive range of motion lacking a few degrees of terminal extension to about one fifteen degrees lacking twenty to thirty degrees of terminal flexion secondary to pain and there is a positive lachman\'s test muscle strength is five out of five and your neurovascular status is intact distally now based on my exam i do think that you may have injured your anterior cruciate ligament and i\'m gon na recommend that we get an mri of your right knee to evaluate the integrity of the acl i\'m gon na prescribe you meloxicam fifteen milligrams a day to help with the swelling and the pain i also want you to use ice and activity modification to help reduce the swelling and the pain i want you to wear a knee immobilizer and use crutches when you\'re walking i also do not want you to jog until we get the results of your mri and we can discuss further treatment options so we will follow up with you once we get the mri results and we can discuss further treatment options\n[patient] okay that sounds good\n[doctor] do you have any questions for me\n[patient] no i think that\'s it\n[doctor] okay great i\'ll have the nurse come in and get you checked out\n[patient] okay sounds good\n[doctor] alright thanks scott }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi scott how are you\n[patient] i\'m doing okay thanks how about you\n[doctor] i\'m doing alright so i see that you\'re here for a right knee injury\n[p