ight radial artery cath site is clean, dry, and intact without hematoma. Radial artery pulses are palpable.\n\nMusculoskeletal\n- Examination: Bilateral lower limb edema. Pitting 1+.\n\nRESULTS\n\nEKG is reviewed and demonstrates a normal sinus rhythm. Good R wave progression. Evolutionary changes that are anticipated after a STEMI.\n\nEchocardiogram is reviewed and demonstrates a reduced ejection fraction of 35%.\n\nASSESSMENT AND PLAN\n\n1. Coronary artery disease.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient has a history of coronary artery disease that we will continue to monitor.\n- Medical Treatment: She will continue aspirin 81 mg daily and Brilinta 90 mg twice daily. She will continue Lipitor 80 mg a day and Toprol 50 mg daily. I am going to refer her to cardiac rehab for education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly.\n\n2. Newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation.\n- Medical Reasoning: A recent echocardiogram revealed a reduced ejection fraction of 35%. She was able to be treated in the cath lab in a timely manner, so I think that the muscle is just stunned. Given her history. it will improve over time.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I advised the patient that she will recover since she is compliant with her medications.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily.\n\n3. Acute heart failure.\n- Medical Reasoning: The patient is retaining fluid.\n- Medical Treatment: I am going to prescribe Lasix 40 mg once a day. I want to add Aldactone 12.5 mg daily. I will order lab work. We will obtain a repeat echo in approximately 2 months.\n\n4. Hypertension.\n- Medical Reasoning: Blood pressure is well controlled. She will tolerate the Aldactone well.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue exercising and to be cognizant of her diet. I explained that there are healthier alternatives available to reduce the amount of preserved meats she consumes.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue lisinopril 20 mg daily. The patient received a referral to nutrition counseling to consider what other diet options might be available to her.\n\n5. Type 2 diabetes.\n- Medical Reasoning: Stable.\n- Patient Education and Counseling: I encouraged the patient to continue monitoring her blood glucose levels.\n- Medical Treatment: Continue metformin as prescribed.\n\nPatient Agreements: The patient understands and agrees with the recommended medical treatment plan.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up as instructed.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] okay so i\'m glad you\'re doing well i know you had a pretty significant heart attack last year and so i\'m glad to see you\'re doing well i\'m gon na tell you a little bit about my assessment and plan so for your first problem your coronary artery disease i know you had a heart attack last year i\'m gon na put you on a heart healthy diet and i\'m gon na refer you to cardiac rehab for some education and to gain confidence to resume exercising regularly so i know you were very upset when you had your heart attack and you stopped exercising and i know you\'re excited to get back to it but i want you to go slow and just take it one step at a time and i\'m gon na give you a referral for cardiac rehab where they\'ll help you get back to exercising so for your second problem your newly reduced left ventricular dysfunction and moderate mitral regurgitation i know you had an echocardiogram that showed a low ejection fraction but i think your heart muscle is just stunned and i think it\'s going to improve over time so i\'m gon na continue you on your lisinopril twenty milligrams a day and i\'m gon na order another echocardiogram in about two months to see how you\'re doing and then for your third problem your heart failure i know you\'ve been retaining a lot of fluid i\'m gon na put you on lasix forty milligrams once a day and i\'m gon na add aldactone twelve point five milligrams a day and i\'m gon na order some blood work to see how you\'re doing and then for your hypertension your blood pressure is well controlled so i\'m gon na continue you on the lisinopril twenty milligrams a day and i\'m gon na refer you to a nutritionist to consider what other diet options might be available to you and for your last problem your type two diabetes i know your blood sugar is stable i\'m gon na continue you on the metformin and i want you to keep monitoring your blood sugar levels so do you have any questions for me\n\n[patient] no not at this time\n\n[doctor] okay good all right thank you and i\'ll see you in two months\n\n[patient] thank you }}<|eot_id|>\n\n###Response B:\n{{ [doctor] hi, miss baker, how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] good, good. so, i know that dr. gomez saw you last time. i know that you had a heart attack. how are you feeling?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay.\n[doctor] okay. so, i reviewed your records, and i know that you\'ve been doing well. i know that you\'ve been taking your medications. i\'m gon na go over a few things with you today. so, i know that you had a heart attack, and you had a heart attack in april. and we put a stent in your heart, and that was, i think, your first heart attack.\n[patient] mm-hmm.\n[doctor] so, i\'m gon na go over a few things with you today. i know that you\'ve been doing well, and i know that you\'ve been taking your medications. so, your blood pressure is well controlled. i know that you\'re taking your aspirin. i know that you\'re taking your brilinta. i know that you\'re taking your toprol. i know that you\'re taking your lisinopril. and i know that you\'re taking your metformin for your diabetes. so, let\'s talk a little bit about your heart. so, i reviewed your ekg today, and i know that you have a normal sinus rhythm. i know that you have a good r wave progression. and i know that we expect some evolutionary changes after a heart attack, so i think that\'s okay.\n[patient] mm-hmm.\n[doctor] a