le bit dizzy and you\'ve been having headaches. have you been having any chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion?\n[patient] no, not really.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been having any muscle aches or any pain in your legs?\n[patient] no, not really.\n[doctor] okay. and how about any dizziness when you stand up quickly?\n[patient] yeah, sometimes i feel a little dizzy when i stand up quickly.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been having any suicidal or homicidal thoughts?\n[patient] no, i\'ve been doing good.\n[doctor] good. so, i know that you have a history of depression. how have you been feeling lately?\n[patient] i\'ve been feeling a little down and i\'ve been having a hard time adjusting since having my heart attack.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been feeling lonely or do you have a support system?\n[patient] i\'m a little lonely.\n[doctor] okay. well, i\'m here to listen. so, let\'s talk a little bit about your lab results. i know that you have a history of stage three chronic kidney disease. your creatinine level looks stable. we\'ll go ahead and order a repeat hemoglobin a1c today. and i\'m going to go ahead and refer you to a dietitian for diabetes education. have you had a hemoglobin a1c recently?\n[patient] yeah, i had one a little while ago and it was sixteen.\n[doctor] okay. and how about your blood pressure?\n[patient] it\'s been good.\n[doctor] okay. great. so, let\'s talk a little bit about your heart disease. i know that you have a history of coronary artery disease and a stent placement at the left anterior descending artery. your stents will remain in place permanently and they typically do not require replacement. it\'s common to experience dizziness when standing quickly and that should resolve once you\'re adjusted to your medications. it\'s important to watch for symptoms such as chest pain or shortness of breath on exertion as that would need further testing such as a stress test. i\'m also going to go ahead and refer you to cardiac rehab.