th the physical exam. i\'m just gon na check the right ankle first. okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so i\'m gon na check the right ankle. i\'m gon na press on different areas, and let me know if it hurts. okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] okay. so i\'m gon na press right here. does that hurt?\n[patient] no, not really.\n[doctor] okay. and how about right here?\n[patient] yes, it does.\n[doctor] okay. all right. so i do see some ecchymosis and there is some swelling over the lateral malleolus. there is tenderness to palpation anterolaterally in the soft tissue. there is no laxity on anterior drawer and inversion stress. there is no bony tenderness on palpation of the foot. neurovascularly, everything looks intact. capillary refill is less than three seconds with a strong dorsalis pedis pulse. sensation is intact to light touch. okay. i\'m gon na go ahead and order some x-rays and we\'ll review those in a moment. does that sound okay?\n[patient] sure.\n[doctor] okay. so i did order three views of the right ankle. and when the results are available, we\'ll review them together. okay?\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] so let\'s talk a little bit about my assessment and plan for you. so it sounds like you have a right ankle sprain. we did not see any evidence of a fracture on your x-ray which is good news. so i\'m gon na recommend that you keep your right lower extremity elevated, especially when you\'re seated. you should also continue to ice it. i\'m gon na give you an air cast today to help stabilize your ankle. you will need to use crutches for the next one to two days. you can start putting weight on it as tolerated after that. for pain control, you can continue to take ibuprofen as needed. you should see significant improvement in your symptoms over the next two weeks. we\'ll follow up in two weeks to see how you\'re doing and to determine your return to play date. does that sound okay?\n[patient] okay. sounds good.\n[doctor] okay. and if you have any changes in you