ping her knees with an ACE bandage. She also takes ibuprofen or Aleve occasionally for pain. She states she tries to be as active as she can. She enjoys running outdoors. The patient adds that she would run 5 to 6 miles per day; however, she is unable to go 0.5 miles secondary to the pain. She denies any previous injuries to her knees.\n\nShe works from home.\n\nMEDICAL HISTORY\n\nPatient reports history of an upper extremity fracture when she was in 6th grade.\n\nREVIEW OF SYSTEMS\n\nMusculoskeletal: Reports bilateral knee pain and crepitus. Denies swelling or redness in the bilateral knees.\n\nVITALS\n\nAll vital signs are within the normal limits.\n\nPHYSICAL EXAM\n\nGAIT: Normal, no deformity present.\nCV: Auscultation of Heart: Regular rate and rhythm. No murmurs.\nNo edema\nNEURO: Patella and Achilles reflexes are symmetrical.\nMSK: Examination of the bilateral lower extremities: Abduction strength is 3/5. Remainder of muscle strength is 5/5. Examination of the bilateral knees: No overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin appreciated. No effusion. Tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. Positive patellar grind test. Knee pain with squatting. Negative Lachman test bilaterally. Negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. Negative McMurray test bilaterally.\n\nRESULTS\n\nX-rays of the bilateral knees were reviewed today. These demonstrate no evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n\nASSESSMENT\n\nBilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome.\n\nPLAN\n\nAfter reviewing the patient\'s examination and radiographic findings today, I have had a lengthy discussion with the patient regarding her current symptoms. I have explained to her that her symptoms are consistent with patellofemoral pain syndrome. We discussed treatment options and I have recommended that we begin with conservative treatment in the form of formal physical therapy to increase her lower extremity strength, mobility, and correct any incorrect running mechanics. I encouraged her to take running easy for now. She can continue taking ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. All questions were answered.\n\nINSTRUCTIONS\n\nThe patient will follow up with me soon.\n#############################\n\n\n###Response A:\n{{ [doctor] hi, sophia. how are you?\n[patient] i\'m doing okay, how about you?\n[doctor] i\'m doing well, thank you. so, i know the nurse told you a little bit about dax, but can you tell me a little bit about what\'s going on with your knees?\n[patient] yeah, so it\'s been about six months now. i\'m having bilateral knee pain.\n[doctor] okay. and is there any specific injury that you remember that caused this?\n[patient] no, i can\'t think of any specific injury.\n[doctor] okay. and can you describe the pain for me?\n[patient] it\'s a deep, achy pain.\n[doctor] okay. and where exactly is the pain located?\n[patient] it\'s on the back of my knees, the back of my patellae.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any swelling or redness in your knees?\n[patient] no, i don\'t have any swelling or redness.\n[doctor] okay. and does the pain get worse with any activities?\n[patient] yeah, it gets worse when i go from sitting to standing, and going up and down the stairs.\n[doctor] okay. and does anything make the pain feel better?\n[patient] yeah, it feels better when i rest.\n[doctor] okay. and have you been doing anything to help with the pain?\n[patient] yeah, i\'ve been icing my knees and wrapping them with an ace bandage.\n[doctor] okay. and are you taking any medications for the pain?\n[patient] yeah, i take ibuprofen or aleve occasionally.\n[doctor] okay. and how active are you?\n[patient] i try to be as active as i can. i love to run outside. i used to run five to six miles a day, but now i can only go like point five miles because of the pain.\n[doctor] okay. and have you had any previous injuries to your knees?\n[patient] no, i haven\'t had any injuries to my knees.\n[doctor] okay. and do you have any other medical problems that we should be aware of?\n[patient] i had a fracture in my arm when i was in the sixth grade.\n[doctor] okay. and are you currently working?\n[patient] yeah, i work from home.\n[doctor] okay. great. well, let me go ahead and do a physical exam on you. i\'m just gon na call out my findings as i go along. so on your gait, your gait is normal. no deformity\'s present. on your cardiovascular exam, your heart has a regular rate and rhythm. no murmurs. no edema on your neuro exam, your patella and achilles reflexes are symmetrical. on your musculoskeletal exam, your bilateral lower extremities, your abduction strength is three out of five. the remainder of your muscle strength is five out of five. on your bilateral knees, there is no overlying erythema, ecchymosis, or warmth of the skin. there is no effusion. there is tenderness to palpation of the bilateral retinaculum. there is positive patellar grind test. knee pain with squatting. negative lachman test bilaterally. negative anterior and posterior drawer tests bilaterally. negative mcmurray test bilaterally.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] i reviewed your x-rays of your bilateral knees today, and they do not show any evidence of any fractures or osteoarthritis.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] so, based on your symptoms and your exam, it sounds like you have bilateral knee patellofemoral pain syndrome. this is a condition that is caused by overuse or overloading of the knee joint, and it can cause pain in the front of the knee and around the patella. it\'s a bit of an overuse injury, and it can be very painful.\n[patient] okay.\n[doctor] so, the treatment for this is typically conservative. i would like to start you out with formal physical therapy to increase your lower extremity strength and mobility and to correct any incorrect running mechanics. i would encourage you to take it easy on the running for now, and you can continue to take ibuprofen or anti-inflammatories as needed for pain. do you have an